From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 02:08:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: tell the Darwin host when a task's mm is shared

The Darwin host's x86 JIT may relax memory ordering while no other agent
can observe an address space, and must keep x86's total store order
while one can. Each guest entry carries a dispatch flag,
DARWIN_UML_DISPATCH_MM_SHARED, for that, and bridge ABI 19 gives it the
meaning below, which a host may rely on.

Another agent can observe an mm while any of three conditions holds,
which current_mm_shared() works out afresh for every guest entry:

- Another task may run on it: mm_users counts more than the entering
  task. A vfork child does not count its parent, which cannot run until
  the child leaves the mm (current->vfork_done is set until then).
- It maps memory that another mm may map too: any VM_MAYSHARE vma.
  That covers shared anonymous memory, shared file mappings, SysV shm,
  memfd, and the io_uring and AIO rings that the kernel fills from
  other CPUs. A read-only shared mapping counts, since its loads must
  keep their order as much as a writer's stores. mmap_region() sets the
  mm's mark through an arch_mmap_shared() hook. When
  vms_complete_munmap_vmas() has removed such a vma (munmap, mremap,
  shmdt, a MAP_FIXED replacement), arch_munmap_shared() walks the vmas
  under mmap_lock and clears the mark if none is left. A fork child has
  the mark only if a shared vma came with it.
- io_uring keeps a ring in the task's own memory (IORING_SETUP_NO_MMAP
  or a provided-buffer ring), which a completion on another CPU or
  another process's IORING_OP_MSG_RING writes. The pinned region
  records its mm, and arch_ring_pin() and arch_ring_unpin() count the
  mm's pinned regions. The unpin, possibly from a work item, comes
  after the ring's last writer.

Once none holds, the next entry lacks the flag, and every store another
agent made before is visible to it: the entering task reads mm_users
and the pin count with acquire loads, which pair with mmput()'s fully
ordered decrement and the unpin's release, and a shared vma is gone
from the page tables before its mark clears. A new sharer always comes
from a system call of a task on the mm (clone() raises mm_users before
the child runs; mmap() and the pin set the mark before they return), so
no entry can miss it. A temporary mm_users reference (procfs, ptrace)
marks entries only while it lasts. The flag describes one entry and is
cleared after it returns, as PSTATE.SS is.

The arm64 host ignores the flag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
